      "content": "The UN often fails to protect specific national interests because its enforcement mechanisms rely on voting equality, allowing a few permanent veto-wielding states to block action against allies of their choosing. Furthermore, the organization historically prioritizes broad geopolitical stability and great-power compromise over the nuanced demands of smaller or specific nations, leading to delayed or diluted responses to crises. Ultimately, the structural design of the body inherently favors collective decision-making and sovereignty over rigid individual advocacy, making it difficult for any single state to force protection at the expense of international consensus.",
